<!--
  aihu-floating-sparkles — presentational styled recipe (performativeUI port,
  Slice 9 — docs/plans/2026-08-01-performative-ui-port.md §Tier-C batch 1).

  Design and behavior transcribed in spirit from performativeUI (MIT); no
  source text or `pui-` class names vendored — see NOTICES.md.

  A decorative ambient particle field — small glowing dots drifting and
  twinkling — built directly on `@aihu/use/motion`'s `useParticleField`
  (Track B Slice 8), which is itself built on `useCanvasSurface`.
  `useCanvasSurface` OWNS canvas creation (see its header comment: measuring
  and styling the same node would feed a `ResizeObserver` a resize loop, so
  it measures the HOST and styles a canvas it creates itself). This
  component's `@template` is therefore a plain container — `ref={hostEl}`
  feeds that host to `useParticleField`, which mounts its own `<canvas>`
  inside it; nothing here reaches for a `<canvas>` element directly.

  count: how many particles, string-encoded number. Default "30".
  color: a single fill color for every particle, default "#ffffff". This is
         a LITERAL CSS color string, not a `--color-*` token reference — every
         other decorative recipe in this port writes `color: var(--color-*)`
         in `@style`, but a canvas 2D context's `fillStyle` is not part of
         the CSS cascade and cannot resolve a custom property; it needs an
         already-resolved value. `useParticleField`'s `colors` option already
         accepts an array (for a future multi-color field); per the roadmap
         this component deliberately does NOT parse a comma-separated string
         into that array yet (over-engineering ahead of a real need) — a
         single-element array is passed instead.

  Layout: a standalone, full-bleed, absolutely-positioned decorative layer
  (`position: absolute; inset: 0`), no slot — meant to sit BEHIND other
  content the same way `aurora` (this slice's sibling component) does, for a
  consistent Tier-C "decorative background layer" contract across the batch.
  The consumer places it as the first child of a `position: relative`
  ancestor and stacks real content above it with `z-index` (see
  `useCanvasSurface`'s header: "the host must be positioned" for its owned
  canvas to land at the host's top-left, which `position: absolute` already
  satisfies here).

  Reduced motion and off-screen pausing are NOT reimplemented here — both are
  the composable family's job. `useCanvasSurface`'s "paint once, never loop"
  contract means `prefersReduced()` true renders one static seeded starfield
  frame instead of looping (traced in `useParticleField`'s `onFrame`: `if
  (!reducedMotion) step(...)` is skipped, `draw(..., !reducedMotion)` turns
  off the twinkle modulation, so the field freezes on its seeded positions —
  never blank). `isVisible`-gated pausing while scrolled off-screen is
  likewise inherited for free.

  `shadow: 'light'` — see the parent execution plan's theming decision.
-->

@state {
  import { cn } from '@aihu/css-engine/runtime/cn'

  shadow: 'light'

  let count = prop({ default: '30' })
  let color = prop({ default: '#ffffff' })

  let hostEl = state(null)

  // Read into a differently-named local before use — an options-object key
  // textually identical to a prop name (`count: count`) trips the compiler's
  // prop-auto-unwrap rewriter (see token-stream.aihu's header for the exact
  // failure mode this sidesteps).
  const particleCount = Number(count)

  useParticleField(() => hostEl, {
    count: particleCount,
    colors: [color],
  })
}

@template {
  <div
    ref={hostEl}
    data-slot="floating-sparkles"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class={cn('aihu-floating-sparkles')}></div>
}

@style {
  .aihu-floating-sparkles {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
}
